<script>
import CompanyPicker from 'crm/src/companies/lib/CompanyPicker.abide'
import companyPickerSource from 'crm/src/companies/lib/CompanyPicker.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import companyPickerTestSource from 'crm/src/companies/lib/CompanyPicker.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import searchCompaniesSource from 'crm/src/companies/searchCompanies.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import ContactPicker from 'crm/src/contacts/lib/ContactPicker.abide'
import contactPickerSource from 'crm/src/contacts/lib/ContactPicker.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import contactPickerTestSource from 'crm/src/contacts/lib/ContactPicker.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import CallQueue from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/CallQueue.abide'
import callQueueSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/CallQueue.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import callQueueTestSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/CallQueue.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A combobox searches a handler as you type">
        <Example
            id="combobox-search"
            title="`Combobox` — an input over the items a search answers"
            summary="A contact's record says where they work. Type part of a company's name or city, move with the arrow keys, and press Enter or click to pick one."
            files={{
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyPicker.abide': companyPickerSource,
                'src/companies/searchCompanies.handler.ts': searchCompaniesSource,
                'src/companies/lib/CompanyPicker.test.ts': companyPickerTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CompanyPicker />
        </Example>

        <p>Import it as <code>import Combobox from '@abide/abide/Combobox.abide'</code>. Hand it
            <code>search</code>, a function of what was typed, and it lists what the function
            answers. A handler call is one. The list is shut until someone types or presses an
            arrow key, and nothing is searched while it is shut. Set <code>debounce</code> to
            wait for the typing to pause before each search.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Prop</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>id</code></td><td>the input's id, for a <code>{'<label for>'}</code>; the list's and each option's ids are built from it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>search</code></td><td>a function of the typed text answering the items, or a promise or reactive value of them</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>options</code></td><td>the items to narrow by their label, where there is no <code>search</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>key</code></td><td>what tells one item from another</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>label</code></td><td>an item's text, shown as its option and put in the input once it is picked</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>bind:value</code></td><td>the item picked, read and set</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>debounce</code></td><td>milliseconds the typing has to pause before <code>search</code> runs again</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>placeholder</code></td><td>the input's placeholder</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>Option</code></td><td>an inline component rendering an option's content, handed <code>item</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>children</td><td>what shows while nothing matches</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Enter picks the active item, and so does a click. Escape shuts the list, and a second
            Escape clears the pick. Moving focus away shuts it too. While a search is in flight the
            last items stay listed. The active item stays active while it is still listed, so a
            narrowing search keeps the arrow keys' place.</p>

        <p>Write the item's type on the parameters of <code>key</code>, <code>label</code> and
            <code>Option</code>, as the card does. An <code>id</code> is required. The ids the
            input's <code>aria-activedescendant</code> and <code>aria-controls</code> point at are
            built from it, and two comboboxes on one page each need their own.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A combobox narrows a list it already has">
        <Example
            id="combobox-options"
            title="`Combobox` with `options` — the items whose label holds what was typed"
            summary="A deal is with one of a handful of contacts, all of them already loaded. Type part of a name to narrow the list, and pick one."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactPicker.abide': contactPickerSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactPicker.test.ts': contactPickerTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactPicker />
        </Example>

        <p>Hand it <code>options</code> in place of <code>search</code>, and it lists each item
            whose <code>label</code> holds the typed text, ignoring case. The options can be a
            reactive value still loading. A narrowed list keeps the rows that are still in it and
            removes the rest, so an option is never built twice while someone types.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`listbox` puts the keyboard on a list you render">
        <Example
            id="listbox-queue"
            title="`listbox(options)` — the active option, the keys and the ids"
            summary="The morning's leads, called one after another. Focus the list, step through it with the arrow keys, and press Enter to call the active lead."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/CallQueue.abide': callQueueSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/CallQueue.test.ts': callQueueTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CallQueue />
        </Example>

        <p><code>listbox</code> is the keyboard <code>Combobox</code> runs on, for markup you
            write yourself. Import it from <code>@abide/abide</code>. Hand it the options as a
            function, so a reactive list is read as it changes, and the key each is told apart by.
            Hand the element that holds focus its <code>keydown</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>ArrowDown · ArrowUp</td><td>the next or the previous option, round the ends unless <code>wrap</code> is <code>false</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td>Home · End</td><td>the first or the last option</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Enter</td><td>calls <code>onpick</code> with the active option</td></tr>
                <tr><td>Escape</td><td>calls <code>onescape</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>The active option is a key. It stays on that option while the list changes around it,
            and falls back to the first option once the list no longer holds it. Read it with
            <code>active()</code> to mark an option, and set it with <code>activate(key)</code>
            from a click or a pointer. <code>idOf(key)</code> is the id an option renders with, and
            <code>activeId()</code> is the active one's, for the list's
            <code>aria-activedescendant</code>. The roles are yours to write:
            <code>role="listbox"</code> on the list, and <code>role="option"</code> with
            <code>aria-selected</code> on each option.</p>

        <p>Each option reads <code>active()</code> to mark itself, so a step wakes every option's
            mark and writes two of them. A list of a few dozen options does not notice. For
            thousands, render only the options in view.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Styling a combobox by its parts and states">
        <p>The combobox ships no styles and writes no class. Every element you can style carries
            a <code>data-part</code>, and every state is a <code>data-</code> attribute present
            while it holds. Any attribute you pass, <code>class</code> included, lands on the
            root.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Part</th><th>States</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>the root <code>div</code></td><td>—</td><td><code>data-open</code> while the list is open, <code>data-busy</code> while a search it waits on is in flight</td></tr>
                <tr><td>the <code>input</code></td><td><code>input</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                <tr><td>the <code>ul</code> of options</td><td><code>listbox</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
                <tr><td>each <code>li</code></td><td><code>option</code></td><td><code>data-active</code> on the active option, <code>data-selected</code> on the picked one</td></tr>
                <tr><td>the message when nothing matches</td><td><code>empty</code></td><td>—</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>It styles</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'[data-part=option][data-active] { … }'}</code></td><td>the active option, from a stylesheet</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="[&_[data-active]]:bg-signal/10"'}</code></td><td>the same, as a Tailwind utility on the root</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="[&_[data-part=listbox]]:list-none"'}</code></td><td>a part, from the root</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="data-[open]:shadow"'}</code></td><td>the root while its list is open</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'class="data-[busy]:opacity-60"'}</code></td><td>the root while a search is in flight</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
